 The browser as a desk pane. The browser is otherwise a standalone program
 that owns the machine, and three of its assumptions have to be answered
 here rather than changed there.

 It normally sets its own video mode. `display-init-text-fallback` hands it
 a DisplayState with `ds-active` False, which makes `display-present` a
 no-op, so the browser never touches the Bochs VBE ports and never writes
 0xBF000000. The desk's framebuffer, from the handoff cells, stays the only
 scanout. Nothing about the browser's rendering is lost by this: the pane
 paints the same `browser-render` tree through `comp-render`, which walks it
 straight to `base`/`stride` and skips the intermediate 786,432-element
 Framebuf the standalone path builds.

 The two keyboards are different alphabets, not different encodings.
 `kbd-take` answers raw Set-1 scancodes; the browser's `map-key-event`
 compares against `key-*` constants and `char-code` values, which is what
 `poll-key` produces. `poll-key-decode` is the existing converter between
 them and is what makes the chords work.

 Modifiers are tracked here because a Set-1 stream carries them as their own
 make and break codes, and the browser wants them as a mask on the event.

Section: Modifier Tracking

  gbr-mod-shift : Integer = 1
  gbr-mod-ctrl : Integer = 2
  gbr-mod-alt : Integer = 4

  gbr-is-mod : Integer -> Boolean
  gbr-is-mod (sc) =
    sc == 29 | sc == 157 | sc == 42 | sc == 170 | sc == 54 | sc == 182 | sc == 56 | sc == 184

  gbr-mods : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  gbr-mods (mods) (sc) =
    if sc == 29 then bit-or mods gbr-mod-ctrl
    else if sc == 157 then bit-and mods 5
    else if sc == 42 then bit-or mods gbr-mod-shift
    else if sc == 54 then bit-or mods gbr-mod-shift
    else if sc == 170 then bit-and mods 6
    else if sc == 182 then bit-and mods 6
    else if sc == 56 then bit-or mods gbr-mod-alt
    else if sc == 184 then bit-and mods 3
    else mods

Section: Pane

 The cursor is drawn INTO the framebuffer over a saved copy of what it
 covers, so a repaint underneath it would leave the save buffer describing
 pixels that no longer exist and the next move would stamp them back. The
 order here is the same one `desk-loop` uses and it is not interchangeable:
 hide (which restores the background), repaint, then update (which saves the
 NEW background and draws).

 The frame mark used to sit inside that bracket and the tree died with it,
 which is why a page link was not clickable: the only tree with real bounds
 on it was the one being thrown away. The tree is kept now, and the mark is
 kept with it in `bs-laid-mark`, so the pane can release exactly this frame's
 allocations later. The mark is taken before `browser-render`, so the raw
 tree is inside the released span too and only the laid-out one is reachable
 afterwards.

 The scroll is fitted BEFORE the frame mark and the paint is done by hand
 instead of through `comp-render`, and both are forced by the same thing. The
 fitted `ScrollState` has to outlive the frame, because every later event
 restores to `bs-laid-mark` and a state allocated above it would be freed while
 the tab still pointed at it. And the page cannot be painted in the same pass as
 the chrome once it moves: shifted up by the offset it would paint over the
 address bar, which the pane clip cannot stop because the address bar is inside
 the pane.

 So the chrome is walked with the viewport subtree skipped, the viewport's own
 box is drawn where the layout put it, and its children are moved by the offset
 and walked again under a clip that is the viewport box narrowed to the pane.
 The narrowing is not decoration: since WORKS-45 the viewport node is taller
 than the space it was given, so its own bounds are not the visible region.

 THE ORIGIN IS THE PANE'S AND THE SIZE IS THE FIT'S. `x` and `y` are DEVICE
 pixels, the top-left of the box this pane was granted, and they move both the
 layout rect and the clip. A windowed browser needs no origin of its own on
 the hit side, because a tree laid out at an absolute rect comes back with
 absolute bounds and `browser-click-code` compares against those
 (`codex/test/apps/desk-window-origin` is where that was settled).

 `browser-scroll-fit` still takes the SIZE alone. Where the box is has nothing
 to do with how much of the page fits in it, and handing it an origin would be
 an argument it could only ignore.

  gbr-repaint : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, BrowserState -> BrowserState
  gbr-repaint (base) (x) (y) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (mouse) (cs) (s) (st) =
    let hd = cursor-hide cs base stride
    in let fit = browser-scroll-fit st (w / s) (h / s)
    in let frame = __heap-save
    in let th = fit.bs-theme
    in let laid = widget-layout (browser-render fit) th (layout-rect (x / s) (y / s) (w / s) (h / s))
    in let d = gbr-paint base stride (layout-rect x y w h) font tf laid th s (browser-scroll-offset fit)
    in let memo = browser-memo-laid fit laid frame
    in let cu = if mouse.um-ok then cursor-update cs base stride w h s (mouse-x mouse) (mouse-y mouse) else 0
    in memo

 The tree handed back is the SHIFTED one, which is what makes a hit test follow
 the scroll: `comp-translate-kids` stores into the nodes rather than copying
 them, so `bs-laid` and the painted geometry are the same records.

  gbr-paint : Integer, Integer, LayoutRect, CbfFont, GopFont, WidgetNode, Theme, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  gbr-paint (base) (stride) (pane) (font) (tf) (laid) (th) (s) (off) =
    when browser-laid-viewport laid
      is None -> comp-walk base stride pane font tf laid th s
      is Just (vp) ->
        let vclip = comp-clip pane (comp-mul-rect (vp.wn-bounds) s)
        in let d1 = comp-walk-except base stride pane font tf laid th s (vp.wn-id)
        in let d2 = comp-draw-node base stride vclip font tf vp th s
        in let mv = comp-translate-kids (vp.wn-children) 0 (0 - off) 0
        in comp-walk-kids base stride vclip font tf (vp.wn-children) th 0 s

 An event releases the memoized tree BEFORE it dispatches, never after. The
 new state a dispatch builds is allocated above the frontier, so a release
 that came second would have to free memory underneath state that must
 survive, which a bump allocator cannot do. That ordering is the whole reason
 `browser-click-code` answers an Integer: the hit has to be read out of the
 tree while it is still alive and still be meaningful once it is gone.

 The idle branches restore `mark` instead, which is ABOVE the tree, so the
 memo survives a poll that found nothing. Steady state is exactly one tree.

Section: Step

 The loop moved to `GopDesk` on 2026-08-18 and this is what replaced it. The
 desk owns the poll and calls `gbr-step` with one event, so the taskbar clock
 keeps time while a page is up. The branch structure is the old loop's,
 unchanged, because its heap discipline is the delicate part: release the
 memoized tree BEFORE dispatching, never after, since the state a dispatch
 builds is allocated above the frontier and a release that came second would
 have to free memory underneath state that must survive.

 `BrowserPane` exists so the pane's two carried values have somewhere to live
 that the desk's per-iteration restore cannot reach.

 **Opening is two calls and that is the whole reason.** `gbr-repaint` takes
 `bs-laid-mark`, and every later event restores to it, so ANYTHING allocated
 after the first paint dies on the pane's first keystroke -- including the
 desk's own `DeskApps` record, which is built by the caller and holds the only
 pointer to this pane. Written as one call it crashed the guest on the third
 scancode, measured, because the desk went on reading a record the browser had
 handed back. So `gbr-new` builds the state and the record, the caller wraps
 them in whatever it keeps them in, and only then does `gbr-first-paint` take
 the mark.

  BrowserPane = record {
    bp-st : BrowserState,
    bp-mods : Integer
  }

  gbr-new : Integer, Integer -> [Device.Port] BrowserPane
  gbr-new (w) (h) =
    let st = browser-init w h browser-theme-default display-init-text-fallback
    in BrowserPane { bp-st = st, bp-mods = 0 }

  gbr-first-paint : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, BrowserPane -> Integer
  gbr-first-paint (base) (x) (y) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (mouse) (cs) (s) (bp) =
    let shown = gbr-repaint base x y w h stride font tf mouse cs s (bp.bp-st)
    in let k = __record-set bp "bp-st" shown
    in 0

 An event answers a negative number, which tells `desk-loop` to keep the frame.
 It has to: the restore above went BELOW the desk's own frame mark, so the desk
 restoring to that mark afterwards would push the frontier back up over memory
 this pane is now using. The idle and modifier branches allocate nothing below
 it and answer 1 as usual.

 The frontier still does not run away, because the release-then-rebuild is the
 old loop's and steady state is one state and one tree. What it creeps by is
 one `BrowserState` per event, which the loop did too.

  gbr-step : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, BrowserPane, Integer, Boolean -> [Device.Port] Integer
  gbr-step (base) (x) (y) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (tf) (mouse) (cs) (s) (bp) (sc) (clicked) =
    let st = bp.bp-st
    in let mods = bp.bp-mods
    in if clicked then
      let mx = comp-hit-x (mouse-x mouse) s
      in let my = comp-hit-y (mouse-y mouse) s
      in let code = browser-click-code st mx my
      in let d = __heap-restore (st.bs-laid-mark)
      in let next = browser-apply-click st mx my code
      in let shown = gbr-repaint base x y w h stride font tf mouse cs s next
      in let k = __record-set bp "bp-st" shown
      in 0 - 1
    else if sc == 0 then 1
    else if gbr-is-mod sc then
      let k = __record-set bp "bp-mods" (gbr-mods mods sc)
      in 1
    else if sc >= 128 then 1
    else
      let key = poll-key-decode sc mods
      in let d = __heap-restore (st.bs-laid-mark)
      in let next = browser-dispatch-event st (event-key-down key mods 0)
      in let shown = gbr-repaint base x y w h stride font tf mouse cs s next
      in let k = __record-set bp "bp-st" shown
      in 0 - 1
